import nbformat as nbf
from pathlib import Path
n=nbf.v4.new_notebook()
M=lambda s:n.cells.append(nbf.v4.new_markdown_cell(s))
C=lambda s:n.cells.append(nbf.v4.new_code_cell(s))
M('''# Mini-Project 5: Uganda Tax Engine\n**MSCS & MSDS — OOP with Python, Assignment 2 (2026)**\n\n**Purpose:** Demonstrate object-oriented tax computation for resident and non-resident PAYE, NSSF, configurable Local Service Tax, VAT, corporate income tax and the TaxPlanner extension. **Educational demonstration, not tax advice.** All currency amounts are UGX.\n\n**Source:** Uganda Revenue Authority (URA) PAYE rates effective 1 July 2026. The assignment is labelled Mini-Project 2, but this repository names it **Project 5** to follow the earlier course projects.''')
M('''## 1. Setup and imports\nRun the notebook from the repository root or its `notebooks` directory. All calculations use `Decimal`, never Python `float`.''')
C('''from pathlib import Path\nimport sys\nROOT = Path.cwd().resolve()\nif ROOT.name == 'notebooks':\n    ROOT = ROOT.parent\nsys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT))\n\nfrom datetime import date\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom src.tax_engine import (Money, ugx, Tax, TaxContext, PAYETax, Payroll,\n    VATTax, Invoice, CorporateIncomeTax, TaxPlanner, InvalidIncomeError,\n    NoScheduleForDateError)\nprint('Loaded Project 5 tax engine from:', ROOT)''')
M('''## 2. OOP: value objects, abstraction, inheritance and polymorphism\n`Money` is immutable and rounds to the nearest whole shilling using `ROUND_HALF_UP`. It overloads `+`, `-`, `*`, `<` and `repr`. `Tax` is an abstract base class requiring `calculate(ctx) -> TaxResult`. PAYE, VAT and corporate income tax each implement that interface. `Payroll` **composes** a PAYE calculator; `TaxPlanner` composes a payroll calculator.''')
C('''a, b = ugx('1000.50'), ugx(500)\nprint('Money rounded:', a, '| sum:', a + b, '| multiplied:', b * Decimal('0.05'))\nprint('Subclass of Tax:', issubclass(PAYETax, Tax), issubclass(VATTax, Tax), issubclass(CorporateIncomeTax, Tax))''')
M('''## 3. PAYE calculations: resident and non-resident\nResident bands: 0–335,000 nil; 335,001–410,000 20% above 335,000; 410,001–485,000 15,000 + 25% above 410,000; 485,001–10m 33,750 + 30% above 485,000; above 10m add 10% of the excess above 10m. Non-resident bands are different: 10% to 335,000; 33,500 + 20% above 335,000 to 410,000; then 48,500 + 30% above 410,000, plus the surcharge above 10m. Values are rounded to whole UGX.''')
C('''paye = PAYETax()\nlevels = [0, 335000, 335001, 410000, 410001, 485000, 485001, 1000000, 10000000, 10000001, 11000000]\nrows = []\nfor income in levels:\n    for residency in ('resident', 'nonresident'):\n        result = paye.calculate(TaxContext(ugx(income), on_date=date(2026,10,1), residency=residency))\n        rows.append({'Monthly taxable income (UGX)': income, 'Residency': residency, 'PAYE (UGX)': int(result.tax.amount)})\npaye_df = pd.DataFrame(rows)\nprint(paye_df.to_string(index=False))''')
M('''## 4. PAYE visualisation\nThe two curves show the different treatment of resident and non-resident taxable employment income. The graph uses integer inputs and converts Decimal tax results to integers **only for plotting**.''')
C('''incomes = list(range(0, 12000001, 250000))\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9,5))\nfor residency in ('resident','nonresident'):\n    taxes = [int(paye.calculate(TaxContext(ugx(x), residency=residency)).tax.amount) for x in incomes]\n    ax.plot(incomes, taxes, label=residency.title())\nax.set(title='Uganda Monthly PAYE by Residency (2026/27)', xlabel='Taxable monthly income (UGX)', ylabel='Monthly PAYE (UGX)')\nax.legend(); ax.grid(alpha=.25); fig.tight_layout(); plt.show()''')
M('''## 5. Payroll pipeline\n**Order:** (1) combine basic pay and taxable allowances; (2) calculate employee NSSF at 5% and employer NSSF at 10% of gross; (3) subtract the **supplied** monthly LST amount from gross to determine PAYE chargeable income; (4) calculate PAYE; (5) deduct employee NSSF, LST and PAYE from gross to get net pay. The employer's 10% NSSF is an additional employer cost, **not** an employee deduction.\n\nURA states that LST is deductible before PAYE. This implementation does **not** subtract employee NSSF from the PAYE base, and it does not attempt to determine the LST assessment automatically. LST is a user-provided deduction, not a fixed monthly percentage.''')
C('''payroll = Payroll()\ncase = payroll.calculate(basic=ugx(1000000), allowances=ugx(150000), lst=ugx(10000), residency='resident')\nfor label, value in vars(case).items():\n    print(f'{label:23s}: {value}')''')
M('''## 6. VAT: standard-rated, zero-rated and exempt\nFor demonstration, invoice amounts are **exclusive of VAT**. Standard-rated supplies attract 18%. Zero-rated supplies attract 0% and may support input VAT recovery under the law; exempt supplies attract no output VAT and input VAT attributable to exempt supplies is not recoverable. This simplified calculator assumes standard-rated purchase input VAT is fully creditable and excludes input VAT on exempt purchases. Mixed-use allocation, imports, tax adjustments and statutory documentation checks are outside scope.''')
C('''invoices = [\n    Invoice('SALE-01','sale','standard',ugx(1000000)),\n    Invoice('SALE-02','sale','zero',ugx(300000)),\n    Invoice('SALE-03','sale','exempt',ugx(200000)),\n    Invoice('BUY-01','purchase','standard',ugx(250000)),\n    Invoice('BUY-02','purchase','exempt',ugx(50000)),\n]\nvat = VATTax().invoices(invoices)\nprint(pd.DataFrame([{'Reference':i.reference,'Direction':i.direction,'Supply':i.category,'Net UGX':int(i.net_amount.amount)} for i in invoices]).to_string(index=False))\nprint('Output VAT:',vat.output_vat,'| Eligible input VAT:',vat.input_vat,'| VAT payable:',vat.vat_payable,'| Credit:',vat.credit_balance)''')
M('''## 7. Corporate income tax\nThe demonstration applies the standard resident-company rate of 30% to positive chargeable income, defined here as revenue minus **allowable** deductions. It does not model losses carried forward, special incentives, alternative minimum taxes or sector-specific regimes.''')
C('''corporate = CorporateIncomeTax().from_accounts(revenue=ugx(120000000), allowable_deductions=ugx(75000000))\nprint('Chargeable income:', corporate.taxable_base)\nprint('Corporate income tax:', corporate.tax)''')
M('''## 8. Extension B: TaxPlanner scenario comparison\nThe planner compares two payroll scenarios under the same PAYE rules and reports the difference in take-home pay. It does not compare salary with dividend taxation because that would require a separate dividend tax model.''')
C('''comparison = TaxPlanner().compare(\n    {'basic':ugx(1000000),'allowances':ugx(0),'lst':ugx(0)},\n    {'basic':ugx(1200000),'allowances':ugx(100000),'lst':ugx(0)}\n)\nprint('Scenario A net:', comparison.first.net_pay)\nprint('Scenario B net:', comparison.second.net_pay)\nprint('B minus A:', comparison.net_pay_difference)''')
M('''## 9. Exceptions and reproducible tests\nInvalid values and unsupported dates raise explicit exceptions. Tests cover band boundaries and include property-based Hypothesis tests in `tests/test_properties.py`.''')
C('''for label, operation in [\n    ('Negative income', lambda: paye.calculate(TaxContext(ugx(-100)))),\n    ('Unsupported date', lambda: paye.calculate(TaxContext(ugx(500000), on_date=date(2025,1,1)))),\n    ('Float money input', lambda: ugx(0.1))\n]:\n    try:\n        operation()\n    except (InvalidIncomeError, NoScheduleForDateError) as exc:\n        print(f'{label}: correctly rejected — {type(exc).__name__}: {exc}')\n    else:\n        raise AssertionError(f'{label} should have failed')\n\nassert paye.calculate(TaxContext(ugx(335000))).tax == ugx(0)\nassert paye.calculate(TaxContext(ugx(410000))).tax == ugx(15000)\nassert paye.calculate(TaxContext(ugx(485000))).tax == ugx(33750)\nassert paye.calculate(TaxContext(ugx(335000), residency='nonresident')).tax == ugx(33500)\nassert vat.vat_payable == ugx(135000)\nprint('Five offline notebook assertions passed.')''')
M('''## 10. Discussion and limitations\n**Why not float?** Binary floating-point cannot exactly represent many decimal fractions (for example, `0.1 + 0.2` is not exactly `0.3`). Financial calculations should use `Decimal` and explicit rounding.\n\n**Where is inheritance justified?** PAYE, VAT and corporate tax share the abstract `Tax.calculate()` contract. **Where is it avoided?** `Payroll` and `TaxPlanner` use composition because payroll is not itself a type of tax and scenario comparison is not a tax.\n\n**Date support:** The configured PAYE rules apply from 1 July 2026 only. Earlier dates raise `NoScheduleForDateError`. A production system needs dated tax schedules, legislative updates and additional exceptions.\n\n**Sources and legal caution:** URA PAYE Rates, URA 7 September 2026 PAYE notice and URA Employment Income guidance; see README for URLs. The standard corporate tax rate is also confirmed by URA's August 2026 Commissioner General Q&A. NSSF rates and standard VAT rate follow the assignment baseline and should be checked against applicable laws for any live filing. This is an educational model, not a substitute for a registered tax adviser or URA's filing tools.\n\n**Academic integrity:** AI assistance was used for code drafting and explanation; review and test every method before submission and explain it in the viva.''')
